# ProjectAI Frontend Rules

ProjectAI frontend is a light analytical Control Center (desktop-first), not a
developer-only admin table and not a trading terminal redesign.

User-facing UI language is Russian.

Do not translate technical identifiers such as:
- SBER
- LKOH
- MOEX
- RUB
- UUID
- model IDs

Backend enums remain technical. Translate them in the presentation layer.

Do not scatter UI labels and enum translations across JSX.
Use centralized presentation mappings/utilities.

Frontend communicates with ProjectAI only through API.

Do not perform heavy analytics in the browser.

API access belongs in the frontend API/data layer, not scattered across components.

Pages must handle:
- loading;
- empty;
- error;
- success.

Prefer readable information hierarchy over dense technical tables.

Technical implementation details may be available in drill-down views but should not
dominate normal user screens.

Preserve existing Control Center patterns when adding sections. Do not redesign the app
unless the task explicitly asks for it.

Avoid:
- giant components;
- unnecessary UI frameworks;
- decorative complexity;
- fake data on unfinished product pages;
- ML/trading language on pages that only prepare data (e.g. datasets without models).

Placeholder sections must clearly say they are not implemented.

Use reusable components where they remove real duplication, but do not build a large
design system prematurely.

Always verify UI changes against real current ProjectAI data, not only mocked tests.

## Help / glossary

Non-obvious metrics and ProjectAI-specific terms shown in the UI must have entries
in the central help registry (`frontend/src/help/registry.ts`).

Use `MetricHelp` / `PageHelp` so essential explanations are available beyond hover
(tooltip summary + click-to-expand drawer). Do not leave raw technical labels without
help when their meaning is not self-evident (e.g. Confidence, RSI14, Relations).
